WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.210
Module eight:

2
00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:02.468
technical analysis.

3
00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:06.174
Candles and timeframes, indicators, patterns and volume,

4
00:00:06.174 --> 00:00:09.350
and which of these the project tested for money.

5
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.772
Technical analysis studies the price chart itself.

6
00:00:13.772 --> 00:00:16.030
Its language is Japanese candles:

7
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:20.478
each shows the open, close, high and low for its stretch of time.

8
00:00:21.478 --> 00:00:24.405
The length of the stretch is the timeframe.

9
00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:28.216
One month is 30 daily candles or 43,200 one-minute ones.

10
00:00:28.216 --> 00:00:34.002
The shorter the candle, the more noise it holds and the more often a strategy trades,

11
00:00:34.002 --> 00:00:36.725
while the fee per trade does not shrink.

12
00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:42.238
That is why on minute charts even ideas that look profitable on hourly ones lose.

13
00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:46.149
An indicator is a formula of past prices:

14
00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:51.260
the 200-day moving average, RSI from zero to a hundred, Bollinger bands,

15
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.751
the Donchian channel.

16
00:00:52.751 --> 00:00:54.028
Notice two things.

17
00:00:54.028 --> 00:00:55.448
Averages always lag.

18
00:00:55.448 --> 00:01:00.204
And RSI can stay overheated for weeks while the price keeps rising.

19
00:01:00.204 --> 00:01:03.470
An indicator retells the past in another form.

20
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.953
Head and shoulders, double bottom, flag:

21
00:01:06.953 --> 00:01:11.050
people are taught to recognize patterns by the shape of the chart.

22
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.588
But the eye finds shapes everywhere, even in coin tosses.

23
00:01:14.588 --> 00:01:18.064
Among these charts there is a real one and random walks.

24
00:01:18.064 --> 00:01:20.982
Telling them apart by eye is almost impossible.

25
00:01:21.982 --> 00:01:26.468
If a pattern also turns up on a random chart, by itself it means nothing.

26
00:01:26.468 --> 00:01:31.323
It only means something if the price behaves differently after the pattern than

27
00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:32.675
after a random moment.

28
00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:33.720
The test is this:

29
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.775
find all the cases over the years, calculate the result with fees,

30
00:01:37.775 --> 00:01:39.742
and compare with random entries.

31
00:01:40.742 --> 00:01:43.149
How do you measure a signal's usefulness?

32
00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:47.141
The project measures the link between the signal and future returns.

33
00:01:47.141 --> 00:01:50.722
Good signals in the stock market give two to five hundredths.

34
00:01:50.722 --> 00:01:53.423
But even a real link may not pay for the fees:

35
00:01:53.423 --> 00:01:58.237
a signal of strength three hundredths gave a difference of under two basis points,

36
00:01:58.237 --> 00:02:00.350
while a trade costs eight to twenty.

37
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.933
The project tested the 200-day moving average, breakouts,

38
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:07.636
Gartley patterns and the Bollinger squeeze.

39
00:02:07.636 --> 00:02:11.407
The moving-average rule cuts drawdown at the cost of return.

40
00:02:11.407 --> 00:02:13.230
The rest does not make money.

41
00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:15.266
The main points.

42
00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:20.513
Candles and indicators compress the past chart but do not predict the future one.

43
00:02:20.513 --> 00:02:23.234
The eye finds patterns even in randomness,

44
00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:26.926
so any pattern is tested on thousands of cases with fees.
